# Review ownership.
#
# WHY THIS FILE IS WORTH GETTING EXACTLY RIGHT: GitHub SILENTLY IGNORES an owner it cannot
# resolve. There is no build failure and no warning in the PR — the rule simply does nothing.
# This file previously read `* @founder-one @founder-two`, neither of which is a real account,
# so it was completely inert while looking like a working policy.
#
# Two more conditions, both easy to miss:
#   1. An owner must have WRITE access to this repo, or the entry is ignored.
#   2. CODEOWNERS only AUTO-REQUESTS review. Blocking a merge on it requires branch protection
#      with "Require review from Code Owners" — which on a private repo needs GitHub Team.
#      Without that plan, everything here is advisory.
#
# HOW TO VERIFY: open this file on github.com. GitHub renders an error banner listing any
# owner it could not resolve. A clean render is the only proof the rules are live.
#
# TODO(satyam): replace the email below with @handle once confirmed. Emails are valid in
# CODEOWNERS, but ONLY when the address is the one GitHub has on file for that account —
# a personal address that is not verified on the GitHub profile resolves to nobody and is
# ignored, exactly like the placeholders this file used to contain. Shivam's handle was
# recovered from his commit noreply address; Satyam has never committed with one, so his
# could not be derived and is deliberately not guessed.
#
# Better long-term: create a `@Forge-in/founders` team in the org and use that. A team
# survives someone changing their username, which an @handle does not.

# Default: both founders review everything. Two people, so this is not a bottleneck yet.
